<BaseLayout title="Troubleshooting" description="ABS delete, heat shielding, and common LS Gen Coupe swap issues.">
  <h1>Troubleshooting &amp; Common Jobs</h1>

  <h2>Heat Shielding</h2>
  <p>
    LS exhaust heat will cook the tunnel and the driver's footwell without shielding.
    DEI stick-on tunnel shielding plus exhaust wrap (or coatings) is strongly recommended.
    Builders report the gas-pedal area becoming uncomfortable or unsafe without both.
  </p>

  <h2>ABS Delete</h2>
  <p>
    Master-cylinder fittings are M12×1 bubble flare. Most remaining OEM brake connections are M10×1 bubble flare.
    Use 3/16&quot; and 1/4&quot; brake line as required and a proportioning valve (port choice off the MC is flexible).
  </p>
  <p>
    Line-lock: correct placement is <strong>after</strong> the proportioning valve so front pressure is held for burnouts while the rears stay free.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Odometer tip:</strong> To keep the dash odometer working, keep the ABS electrical connector plugged in even if the hydraulic unit is removed — unbolt the connector from the ABS housing and leave it electrically connected.
  </p>

  <h2>Push-to-Start Starter Run-On</h2>
  <p>
    Factory logic cuts the starter when it sees engine RPM. After the swap that signal path is gone, so the starter can stay engaged.
    Community fixes include an Arduino reading cleaned tach and driving a relay, or other RPM-based cutout schemes (see Wiring).
  </p>

  <h2>Cluster / Gauges</h2>
  <p>
    Speedometer and fuel gauge are the usual first wins. Tach and coolant temp often need CAN work or a stand-alone dash (Holley).
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
